NNAAMMEE
     ssttrrcchhrr, ssttrrrrcchhrr, ssttrrcchhrrnnuull — locate character in string

LLIIBBRRAARRYY
     Standard C Library (libc, -lc)

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ##iinncclluuddee <<ssttrriinngg..hh>>

     _c_h_a_r _*
     ssttrrcchhrr(_c_o_n_s_t _c_h_a_r _*_s, _i_n_t _c);

     _c_h_a_r _*
     ssttrrrrcchhrr(_c_o_n_s_t _c_h_a_r _*_s, _i_n_t _c);

     _c_h_a_r _*
     ssttrrcchhrrnnuull(_c_o_n_s_t _c_h_a_r _*_s, _i_n_t _c);

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ssttrrcchhrr() function locates the first occurrence of _c (converted to a
     _c_h_a_r) in the string pointed to by _s.  The terminating null character is
     considered part of the string; therefore if _c is ‘\0’, the functions lo‐
     cate the terminating ‘\0’.

     The ssttrrrrcchhrr() function is identical to ssttrrcchhrr() except it locates the
     last occurrence of _c.

     The ssttrrcchhrrnnuull() function is identical to ssttrrcchhrr() except that if _c is not
     found in _s a pointer to the terminating ‘\0’ is returned.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The functions ssttrrcchhrr() and ssttrrrrcchhrr() return a pointer to the located
     character, or NULL if the character does not appear in the string.

     ssttrrcchhrrnnuull() returns a pointer to the terminating ‘\0’ if the character
     does not appear in the string.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     memchr(3), memmem(3), strcspn(3), strpbrk(3), strsep(3), strspn(3),
     strstr(3), strtok(3), wcschr(3)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The functions ssttrrcchhrr() and ssttrrrrcchhrr() conform to ISO/IEC 9899:1990
     (“ISO C90”).  The function ssttrrcchhrrnnuull() is a GNU extension.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ssttrrcchhrrnnuull() function first appeared in glibc 2.1.1 and was added in
     FreeBSD 10.0.

